\paragraph{Accuracy and difference.} Both methods are close to the ceiling
(Table~\ref{tab:results}). LR is ahead on average by \DIFFmean{} pp: it is
better on \FoldsLRbetter{} folds, worse on \FoldsKNNbetter{} and tied on
\FoldsTied{} (Figure~\ref{fig:acc}b). The corrected interval for the
difference, \DIFFlo{} to \DIFFhi{} pp, contains 0 (two-sided $p=\DIFFp$), so
we find no detectable difference between the methods on Iris. The naive
interval (\DIFFloNaive{} to \DIFFhiNaive{} pp) would exclude 0 only because it
ignores the overlap of the training sets. The inner cross-validation chose $k$
between \Kmin{} and \Kmax{} (median \Kmedian, most often \Kmode). A grid
$k\in\{1,\dots,30\}$ gave the same conclusion (\texttt{code/README.md}).

\paragraph{Local versus Ibex.} The two runs are identical on two different CPU
models (Intel and AMD, both x86-64): per-fold results and predictions have equal SHA-256 hashes, so
the chosen $k$ and all accuracies agree (\texttt{results/summary.json}).

\paragraph{Limitations.} Iris is small and nearly separable, so one
misclassified test flower moves a fold's accuracy by $1/\Ntest$ and most
folds tie. The corrected interval is an approximation, not an exact test;
conclusions apply to this dataset and these settings only. Identity is shown for this pinned environment.

